\section{Keep the Coordinate Systems Separate}

Levine distinguishes adjusting a target for its placement from resolving a reference to a symbol, and shows how a section symbol allows a linker to express both operations uniformly. His historical \texttt{a.out} examples have tentative segment bases in the input; they are not a recipe for decoding ELF records. For relinkable output, his key observation is that combining sections moves the relocation place within the new section and can change the representation of its target \cite[chapter~7]{levine2000linkers}.

In an ELF relocatable object, the relocation's \verb|r_offset| is an offset in the section being patched. The symbol field indexes the associated symbol table, not the table of input sections. A section-defined symbol's value in this kind of object is also section-relative, but it is relative to \emph{its own} section. ELF \verb|RELA| stores the addend separately; for \verb|REL| the ABI tells the linker how to recover an implicit addend from the field. The \emph{System V AMD64 Architecture Processor Supplement}, Table 4.10 in the draft cited here, specifies $S+A-P$ for the four-byte \verb|R_X86_64_PC32| and $S+A$ for the eight-byte \verb|R_X86_64_64|. In the final static executable below, $S$ and $P$ are both virtual addresses and $A$ is a signed byte adjustment. The file offset of a field is not its $P$.

\section{Rebase the Place and Preserve the Target Expression}

If an input's field is at offset $p$ and that input section begins at offset $u$ in its new section, emit the record at $p'=u+p$. Independently, if a relocation names an input section symbol of value zero and its contribution starts at offset $v$ in the new target section, a reference expressed against the new section symbol needs $A'=A+v$. If the linker instead preserves an ordinary named symbol with its value adjusted by $v$, the addend should not also receive $v$. The two representations describe the same target; adjusting both counts the target movement twice. Preserve the relocation type unless there is a justified transformation. In particular a reference to an undefined symbol, or to a definition whose selection can change at the final link, cannot just be baked into the bytes because it happens to have a known value during a partial link.

