\section{Partial-Link a Data Displacement and a Pointer}

Use these inputs with GNU \texttt{as} for x86-64 ELF. The displacement is relative to the field itself; this is a data convention and has no instruction-length bias.

\begin{verbatim}
# prefix.s: move the source and target contributions differently.
.section .idx,"a",@progbits
.space 0x0c
.section .records,"a",@progbits
.space 0x20

# delta.s: offsets 5 and 9 in this input's .idx section.
.section .idx,"a",@progbits
.space 5
.long .Lrecord + 3 - .      # Field start is the consumer's base.
.quad .Lrecord + 3          # Same target, but no subtraction.
.section .records,"a",@progbits
.space 7
.Lrecord: .space 8, 0x5a
\end{verbatim}

Assemble separately and inspect \verb|readelf -rW delta.o|. The section symbol \texttt{.records} has an addend of \verb|0xa| for both entries: seven to reach the record and three more to select a byte within it. Run \verb|ld.bfd -r -o packed.o| with \texttt{prefix.o} followed by \texttt{delta.o}. The place offsets in \texttt{.idx} should become \verb|0x11| and \verb|0x15|, respectively, while the addend becomes \verb|0x2a| for the rebased section symbol. Nothing here treats section-relative offset \verb|0x11| as the final virtual address of a field.

Use this final placement script with \verb|ld.bfd -T delta.ld -o packed packed.o|, then link the two original inputs in the same order under the same script into a second image.

\begin{verbatim}
SECTIONS {
  .records 0x407080 : { *(.records) }
  .idx     0x407100 : { *(.idx) }
}
\end{verbatim}

The relative entry now has $S=\mathtt{0x407080}$, $A=\mathtt{0x2a}$, and $P=\mathtt{0x407111}$. Thus $S+A-P=-\mathtt{0x67}$, encoded as \verb|99 ff ff ff| in little-endian. The absolute entry holds $S+A=\mathtt{0x4070aa}$, encoded as \verb|aa 70 40 00 00 00 00 00|. Compare the \texttt{.idx} bytes in both images with \verb|objdump -s -j .idx|; the input offset, intermediate offset, and final virtual address explain three views of the same field.

\Cref{fig:visual-288} follows the four-byte field and its target through two independent coordinate conversions.

