\section{Test the Algebra, Not Just the Hex Dump}

Raise both output section bases by \verb|0x1000| and repeat. $S$ and $P$ change by the same amount, so the relative field is unchanged and the absolute field increases by \verb|0x1000|. Instead, move \texttt{.records} to \verb|0x90000000| without moving \texttt{.idx}; the signed displacement exceeds the four-byte range and the final link must diagnose it. A partial object still carries information for that check; it has not promised a valid placement.

The invariant is that, under equivalent final placement and binding, directly linking a retained relocation and routing it through a partial link evaluate to the same expression at the same final field. It is not a promise that arbitrary one-stage and two-stage link lines have identical layouts or admission decisions. The partial link can already have selected archive members, folded contributions together, or chosen a prevailing group. Compare explicit inputs and known section placements first so the test isolates coordinate conversion rather than those policy changes.

In an implementation, this rebasing rule is a special case of mapping each input byte offset to its output offset. Concatenation with fixed padding gives one constant per contribution. If the linker merges individual string entries or rewrites instructions to new sizes, a constant may no longer suffice. Apply the source mapping to the field and the target mapping to the referenced location; do not assume the two mappings are identical just because the original references were both section-relative. GNU \texttt{ld}'s \verb|--emit-relocs| on a final executable can retain records for inspection or post-link tools; it does not turn the executable back into an \verb|ET_REL| input.
